From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 14:41:04 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: hci_vhci: do not let the core suspend a vhci
 controller

The controller behind a vhci device belongs to the userspace bridge that
created it. On LuneOS that is bluebinder in front of the vendor's Android
Bluetooth HAL, which already handles system suspend the way the vendor
expects.

hci_suspend_notifier() still runs for it. On every suspend attempt it
sends Set Event Filter, Write Scan Enable and the LE scan parameter and
enable commands, and on resume the same four again. On MediaTek phones
with the connac1x Bluetooth driver (bt_drv_connac1x), each reply from the
firmware goes through BT_event_cb(), which takes a 100 ms wakeup event
on bt_drv. The reply lands inside the suspend window, so freezing aborts
with "Pending Wakeup Sources: bt_drv" and the next attempt repeats it:

  Q25 (MT8781, 5.10): 0 suspends and 233 failures in 5 hours; 40
  suspends in 18 minutes with Bluetooth soft-blocked.
  Brax3 (MT6835, 5.15): 0 suspends and 3623 failures in 2 days 5 hours.

btmon showed the four commands and their replies at each of 17 attempts,
matching 17 new bt_drv activations.

Set HCI_QUIRK_NO_SUSPEND_NOTIFIER on vhci controllers so the notifier is
not registered for them, which is what stock Android gets by keeping its
Bluetooth stack in userspace. The suspend_notifier parameter brings the
old behaviour back (hci_vhci.suspend_notifier=1 on the kernel command
line, since the driver is usually built in).

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [LuneOS bluebinder/vhci setup]
---
 drivers/bluetooth/hci_vhci.c | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_vhci.c b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_vhci.c
index 31d70bad8..9670ebd4d 100644
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_vhci.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_vhci.c
@@ -28,6 +28,7 @@
 #define VERSION "1.5"
 
 static bool amp;
+static bool suspend_notifier;
 
 struct vhci_data {
 	struct hci_dev *hdev;
@@ -126,6 +127,18 @@ static int __vhci_create_device(struct vhci_data *data, __u8 opcode)
 
 	set_bit(HCI_QUIRK_VALID_LE_STATES, &hdev->quirks);
 
+	/*
+	 * The controller behind a vhci device belongs to the userspace bridge
+	 * that created it (bluebinder in front of an Android Bluetooth HAL),
+	 * and that side already handles system suspend the way the vendor
+	 * expects. If the core also reconfigures the controller around each
+	 * suspend, the replies can arrive inside the suspend window: MediaTek's
+	 * bt_drv takes a 100 ms wakeup event for every packet the firmware
+	 * sends, so every attempt aborts and the device never suspends.
+	 */
+	if (!suspend_notifier)
+		set_bit(HCI_QUIRK_NO_SUSPEND_NOTIFIER, &hdev->quirks);
+
 	if (hci_register_dev(hdev) < 0) {
 		BT_ERR("Can't register HCI device");
 		hci_free_dev(hdev);
@@ -373,6 +386,10 @@ module_misc_device(vhci_miscdev);
 module_param(amp, bool, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(amp, "Create AMP controller device");
 
+module_param(suspend_notifier, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(suspend_notifier,
+		 "Let the Bluetooth core reconfigure the controller around system suspend");
+
 MODULE_AUTHOR("Marcel Holtmann <marcel@holtmann.org>");
 MODULE_DESCRIPTION("Bluetooth virtual HCI driver ver " VERSION);
 MODULE_VERSION(VERSION);
-- 
2.53.0

